# Fixed defect 131's class, 2026-09-29, beside
# `fixedbugs-131-an-arm-broken-before-its-arrow`: three more ways to break an
# arm before its `=>`. Symptom: `0 7` over `=> 5` cost `expected_arm_arrow`
# at the `7` and `expected_pattern` at the `=>` below; `_` over a `=> 5` at
# the `match`'s own margin, past the arms' dedent, cost `expected_arm_arrow`
# and then `expected_expression` at the `=>`; and `0` over a `=>` alone at
# its line's end, which the lexer joins to the next arm, cost the lexer's
# `continuation_outside_brackets` and `expected_arm_arrow`. Cause: the arm
# went on at a `=>` below only where its line ended right after the pattern,
# and inside the arms' block. Now it goes on past the junk its report names,
# and past the arms' own dedent, whose line then ends the arms; and a `=>`
# the lexer joined past holds its missing arrow back against the lexer's
# refusal, said in the control arm, `check --permissive`, which drops it
# (`selfhost/parse/broken_arm.hero`).

function junk_before_the_break(n: i64) -> i64
    return match n
        0 7  #~ expected_arm_arrow
        => 5
        _ => 20

function an_arrow_past_the_arms(n: i64) -> i64
    k = match n
        _  #~ expected_arm_arrow
    => 5
    return k

function an_arrow_alone(n: i64) -> i64
    return match n
        0
        #~v continuation_outside_brackets
        =>
        _ => 20

function main()
    print(junk_before_the_break(n: 0))
    print(an_arrow_past_the_arms(n: 0))
    print(an_arrow_alone(n: 0))
